# Beats 10 and 11 of grpconf/synopsis.md — 4:30 + 1:30, merged.
#
# Payload:  grpconf/stage/boblog, 20 243 bytes — the same file beat 9
#           just closed.
# Database: grpconf/stage/bobapp2.desc — 130 044 B, 77 files, recovered
#           from the SECOND binary Bob downloaded.
#
# Invocation:
#   protolens --descriptor-set $DESC2 -I $SRC2 \
#       --script grpconf/beats/log-v2.script $BOBLOG
#
# One command apart from beat 9, and the command is the beat: the only
# thing that changed is --descriptor-set.  Four moves, in this order.
#
#   1. The opaque halves have names now       (Places request and reply)
#   2. The field that occurs twice, and the key inside it
#   3. The tie — where the tool stops and defers   (Routes v1 vs v2)
#   4. The reconstructed source earns its keep, then w + four anomalies
#
# Then beat 11: export to prototext.  The export must run in THIS
# session, because it has to carry the overrides the presenter set in
# beat 10 — that is why the two beats are one script (synopsis, "The
# protolens invocations").  The cmp stays a shell step.
#
# The five override: directives below PREFILL and do NOT execute.  The
# presenter presses Enter for each, and ANNOUNCES it: a flawless
# instantaneous demo reads as a recording, and work shown is proof.
#
# Positional paths (root type is known under this database too):
#   /1 /2   Places entries      /3 /4   Routes entries; /4 is the cut one
#   :1 at   :2 method   :3 note   :4 request   :5 response
#   /1/4/1  the first of the two text_query occurrences — the leak
#
# Re-pin:
#   protolens --descriptor-set $DESC2 -I $SRC2 \
#       --script grpconf/beats/log-v2.script $BOBLOG script \
#     | grep 'error:'
#
# TWO error lines are expected there, and only two:
#
#   no match for "val_ohb"      (the node:, and again the wire_line:)
#
# The headless transcript applies node:/fold:/wire: but never executes
# a prefill, so the route request is still an escaped byte string when
# that step is reached and the annotation the search is looking for does
# not exist yet.  That one anomaly is the only one with no ASCII to
# anchor on: it is five padding bytes.  Every other search below is
# deliberately spelled so that it matches in BOTH states — the text is
# visible inside the escaped bytes before the override and on its own
# leaf after it.  A third error line is real drift.

title: The newer build

steps:
  - text: |
      Same file.  Same tool.  One command apart.

      The database is the one built from the binary Bob downloaded
      second — the one whose extra thirty-six files we read off a diff
      in beat 5 and then deliberately did not explain.

      Press space, then -> to step.
    fold: ["/ 1"]
    node: /

  # ---------------------------------------------------------------
  # MOVE 1 — the opaque halves have names now
  # ---------------------------------------------------------------

  - text: |
      Start where beat 9 gave up.

      These 240 bytes scored -26 against GeneratedCodeInfo.Annotation
      a minute ago.  Under this database the cue says

          google.maps.places.v1.SearchTextRequest    -8

      Same bytes, same tool.  The command line is prefilled; press
      Enter.
    fold: ["/ 1", "/1 Z"]
    node: /1/4
    override: override /1/4 --as google.maps.places.v1.SearchTextRequest

  - text: |
      A flat blob becomes a tree.

      text_query: "coffee in Grenoble", a language code, and a
      location_bias.circle around a lat/lng.

      Bob's app does more than he thought.  It does not just route; it
      geocodes, and it sends a place name and a coordinate to a second
      Google service on every run.

      The reply below went from a three-way tie at -50 to +45.  Enter
      opens it too.
    fold: ["/ 1", "/1 1", "/1/4 Z"]
    node: coffee in Grenoble
    override: override /1/5 --as google.maps.places.v1.SearchTextResponse

  - text: |
      Five Grenoble coffee shops, with their coordinates and their
      ratings.

      Worth one sentence and no more: this reply is a tie as well,
      +45 between SearchTextResponse and SearchNearbyResponse.  The
      method: line two entries up already told us which.  Hold that
      thought — move 3 is built on it.
    fold: ["/ 1", "/1 1", "/1/5 Z"]
    node: Hono Coffee House

  # ---------------------------------------------------------------
  # MOVE 2 — the field that occurs twice, and what is in front of it
  # ---------------------------------------------------------------

  - text: |
      Now look again at the request, and count the text_query fields.

      SearchTextRequest declares string text_query = 1 — singular.  It
      is here twice.  That is legal on the wire, and the specification
      says last one wins, so protoc --decode prints "coffee in
      Grenoble" and drops the other one without a word.

      Both are on the wire, so both are here, in wire order.
    fold: ["/ 1", "/1 1", "/1/4 Z"]
    node: coffee in Grenoble

  - text: |
      And the first one is not a place name.

      It is 164 bytes of message wearing a string.  The field is
      declared string, so nothing in the schema invites a second look —
      but the cue is scored on the bytes, not on the declaration, and
      under this database those bytes have exactly one candidate:

          google.rpc.Status    +8    (no runner-up)

      Press Enter to take it at its word.
    fold: ["/ 1", "/1 1", "/1/4 Z"]
    node: "debug: outbound call authenticated"
    override: override /1/4/1 --as google.rpc.Status

  - text: |
      Open it, and the Any says what it is carrying, in plain text:

          type.googleapis.com/google.rpc.ErrorInfo

      and inside that, the metadata bobapp attached to its own debug
      trace:

          "x-goog-api-key": "AIza..."

      This program writes Bob's credential into Bob's own log, in a
      field a normal decoder never prints.  That is the finding, and
      it is the reason this talk exists.
    fold: ["/ 1", "/1 1", "/1/4 Z"]
    node: x-goog-api-key

  - text: |
      Then point at the score, because it is the cheapest quantitative
      claim in the demo.

      Under bobapp1.desc these same 164 bytes were +3, in a three-way
      tie, and the Any inside stayed an opaque escaped string.  Here
      they are +8, sole, and the Any expands.

      The five points are the five fields inside it — reason, domain,
      metadata, and the entry's key and value — readable only because
      the newer build happened to embed google/rpc/error_details.proto.
      That was the third file in beat 5's diff, and now we know what it
      was for.

      More schema, more the tool can say.  Not a slogan; a subtraction.
    fold: ["/ 1", "/1 1", "/1/4 Z"]
    node: x-goog-api-key

  # ---------------------------------------------------------------
  # MOVE 3 — the tie, where the tool stops and defers
  # ---------------------------------------------------------------

  - text: |
      Now the route request in the same log.  84 bytes — the payload
      beat 8 answered outright.

      Under this database the picture changed.  Two candidates, the
      same score:

          google.maps.routes.v1.ComputeRoutesRequest     -16
          google.maps.routing.v2.ComputeRoutesRequest    -16

      They render this payload identically, field for field.  v1's
      field numbers are a strict subset of v2's, so v1 can never
      outrank v2; a tie is the ceiling.  The tool is not confused, and
      it did not rank badly.  On these bytes the two published schemas
      genuinely say the same thing.
    fold: ["/ 1", "/3 Z"]
    node: /3/4

  - text: |
      What breaks the tie is not in the payload at all.  It is two
      lines up, in the envelope:

          method: "/google.maps.routing.v2.Routes/ComputeRoutes"

      A string, in the same file, that no scoring function is allowed
      to read.  Read it out loud and pick v2 by hand.  That is the
      point: it ranks, then it defers.

      Press Enter to commit the choice.

      Say the rhyme once, here: three times now — the method: line in
      beat 9, the type_url inside the Status, and this line — the file
      has told us something the schema could not.  Producers put names
      on the wire.
    fold: ["/ 1"]
    node: routing.v2.Routes/ComputeRoutes
    override: override /3/4 --as google.maps.routing.v2.ComputeRoutesRequest

  - text: |
      And say the other half, because it is the honest half.

      This tie only exists under the newer database.  bobapp1.desc had
      never heard of routes.v1, which is exactly why beat 8 was
      unambiguous.

      So the escalation bought two opposite things in one command:
      names for the Places payloads and for the key, and a genuine
      ambiguity about traffic that was never in doubt before.  Bigger
      dictionaries do not monotonically help.  Beat 12 pushes that one
      notch further.
    fold: ["/ 1", "/3 1", "/3/4 Z"]
    node: /3/4

  # ---------------------------------------------------------------
  # MOVE 4 — the reconstructed source, then the wire
  #
  # Deliberate stillness.  Automation can deliver a three-tier wire
  # display in 200 ms; nobody absorbs it in 200 ms.  The commentary
  # lands before each move or after it, never during.
  # ---------------------------------------------------------------

  - text: |
      Before accepting a candidate for a subnode, press v on the field.

      It opens that type's definition in the .proto reproto wrote in
      beat 6 — the same editor, the same file, the one beat 7 was
      setup for.  Judging whether a type really fits is not something a
      score can do for you, and what you want in front of you while you
      do it is the schema, as source, in your own editor.

      Binary, to descriptors, to source, and back into the source from
      the payload.  Leave with :q.
    fold: ["/ 1", "/3 1", "/3/4 Z"]
    node: en-US

  - text: |
      Now w, and the bytes each line was decoded from appear underneath
      it, in three severity tiers.  Four anomalies follow.

      1.  The field that occurs twice.  Both occurrences, in wire
      order, back in the first entry.

          protoc --decode shows you one value.  prototext shows you
          both, because both are on the wire.  And the first one is
          Bob's API key.
    fold: ["/ 1", "/1 1", "/1/4 Z"]
    node: coffee in Grenoble
    wire_line: coffee in Grenoble

  - text: |
      2.  A non-minimal varint.

          20 81 80 80 80 00

      where two bytes would do.  It decodes to the same number it would
      without the padding and re-encodes to different bytes.  Nothing
      downstream notices: no parser errors, no field changes value, and
      the difference survives every hop.

      This is where "non-canonical" stops being an abstraction.  It is
      a color on a byte — and it is the payoff for the small odd thing
      beat 4 noticed in passing, and for one of the two deductions beat
      8 priced.
    fold: ["/ 1", "/3 1", "/3/4 Z"]
    node: val_ohb
    wire_line: val_ohb

  - text: |
      3.  A field the recovered schema does not declare.

          99: "bobapp/0.9.3-rc2"

      A newer producer against an older schema.  Nothing is lost: the
      wire type is in the tag, so the shape is readable even though the
      name is not, and it is named here by what it is rather than by
      what it is called.

      This is the one an ordinary decoder drops on the floor, and it is
      the other deduction from beat 8.
    fold: ["/ 1", "/3 1", "/3/4 Z"]
    node: bobapp/0.9.3-rc2
    wire_line: bobapp/0.9.3-rc2

  - text: |
      4.  The truncated record — back where beat 9 started.

      Where protoc gives up, and where the length prefix bounds the
      damage to one entry out of four.  MISSING: 1024, recorded rather
      than guessed at.

      Hold this one on screen.  It is the thing the next beat has to
      re-encode.
    fold: ["/ 1"]
    node: /4

  # ---------------------------------------------------------------
  # BEAT 11 — what Alice sends back
  # ---------------------------------------------------------------

  - text: |
      One question left.  We have read the payload, named its fields,
      and pointed at four things standard tooling does not show.

      Did we change any of it?

      Press Enter to write the document out as plain text — an ordinary
      annotated file that Bob can open in any editor with nothing
      installed.  That is what Alice sends back, and the demo has a
      recipient, which is a better ending than a checksum.
    fold: ["/ 1"]
    node: /
    override: export grpconf/stage/boblog.prototext

  - text: |
      The proof is one command in the shell:

          prototext encode boblog.prototext -o roundtrip.pb
          cmp boblog roundtrip.pb && echo identical

      Byte for byte — including the 1 024 bytes that are missing from
      the end of it, which the annotation records rather than invents.

      The leaked key survived.  It would not have survived
      protoc --decode.

      :quit, and we close on the shell.
    fold: ["/ 1"]
    node: /
